Q: null vs undefined: when do you use each?
answer: undefined - set automatically by js engine for a variable for uninitialized variables or missing parameters, functions with no return values etc. We generally do not set undefined to variables ourselves
null - set by developer. To be used when we want to indicate an empty value.

Q: Allowed Patch Statuses

answer: What is patch? Patch is used to partially update a record.
Different patch statuses: 200 -> success and record is returned, 204 -> success and record is not returned, 400 -> bad request malformed, 404 -> user not found, 409 -> the records added conflict, 429 -> too many requests

code: 
const express = require("express");
const server = express();
server.use(express.json());
const PORT = 3000;
const data = [
  {
    id: 1,
    name: "Suruchi",
    email: "suruchi2@salesforce.com",
    age: 44,
    section: "B",
  },
  {
    id: 2,
    name: "Ashutosh",
    email: "ashutosh@freshworks.com",
    age: 34,
    section: "A",
  },
];

const ALLOWED = ["name", "email", "age", "section"];

server.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log("Server is listening on Port " + PORT);
});

server.patch("/users/:id", (req, res) => {
  const id = req.params.id;
  const patchData = req.body;

  const record = data[id - 1];

  if (!record) {
    return res.status(404).json("No record with id " + id);
  }

  const badEntry = Object.keys(patchData).filter(
    (key) => !ALLOWED.includes(key),
  );

  if (badEntry.length > 0) {
    return res.status(400).json("Malformed data sent");
  }

  if (data.some((d) => d.email == patchData.email && d.id !== record.id)) {
    return res.status(409).json("Same email already exists");
  }

  Object.assign(record, patchData);
  res.status(200).json(record);
  // res.sendStatus(204);
});

