Q: What is Cluster?

answer:
Using cluster processes we spawn multiple process on multi core so as to serve multiple requests concurrently. The processes are assigned 
to requests in a round robin fashion by the primary process (default MACOs/ Linux). We can choose the number of cpus as the number of 
processes. The primary process is on one of the cpus assigned by the OS since mostly idle.
We can use PM2 if we dont want to create clusters ourself.
We can install globally and use pm2 start app.js -i max    # one process per CPU core

const cluster = require("cluster");
const CPUs = require("os").availableParallelism;

if (cluster.isPrimary) {
  for (let i = 1; i <= CPUs; i++) {
    cluster.fork();
  }

  cluster.on("exit", () => cluster.fork());
} else {
  const express = require("express");
  const app = express();

  app.get("/", (req, res) => {
    res.send(`Listening on port 3000 ${process.pid}`);
  });

  app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log(`Listening on port 3000 ${process.pid}`);
  });
}

Q: What are worker threads

answer:
1. We use worker threads when we want to perform some synchronous blocking non IO operations since it would not go the the libuv threads. 
For eg: adding a billion numbers
2. For this we use a worker thread which can run the worker file in a separate thread but under same process in a free core decided by OS 
to achieve parallelism.
3. We get a worker object in the main thread so as to get a handle on the threads call callabacks on events like message or error.
4. We use parentPort.postMessage to send data back to the main thread.
5. Each thread gets its own memory and event loop but not a different process.

code:

const {
  Worker,
  isMainThread,
  workerData,
  parentPort,
  threadId,
} = require("worker_threads");

if (isMainThread) {
  // worker is a Worker object, a handle the main thread uses to control and talk to the new thread the object key has to be workerData nodejs worker constructor looks for it.
  const worker = new Worker(__filename, { workerData: 40 });

  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    console.log(`Running the main thread ${process.pid} ${threadId}`);
  }, 100);

  // callback to run on post message
  worker.on("message", (data) => {
    clearInterval(interval);
    console.log(`Result from worker ${data}`);
  });

  // callback to run on error
  worker.on("error", (err) => {
    clearInterval(interval);
    console.log(err);
  });

  worker.on("exit", () => clearInterval(interval));
} else {
  console.log(
    `Data passed by parent ${workerData}, ${process.pid} ${threadId}`,
  );
  const fib = (n) => (n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 2) + fib(n - 1));
  parentPort.postMessage(fib(workerData));
}

Question: Cluster v/s Worker

answer:
Cluster:
 Each worker is a new OS process, so it is heavier.
 Each has a different process id.
 Processes do not share memory; each has its own V8 instance and event loop.
 They communicate using inter-process communication (IPC) messages.
 The OS chooses the core.
 Each process has its own libuv thread pool.
 Usage: handle more requests in parallel across cores.
 All workers share the same port.
 A crashed process is fully isolated; the primary can respawn it
 (cluster.on("exit", () => cluster.fork())).

Worker Thread:
 Runs in the same process, so it is lighter.
 Same process id.
 Each thread has its own V8 instance, JS heap and event loop. Threads communicate with messages (postMessage / workerData, data is copied) 
 and can optionally share raw memory with SharedArrayBuffer (use Atomics to avoid race conditions).
 The OS chooses the core.
 The libuv thread pool is shared by all threads in the process.
 Usage: CPU-heavy work, so the main thread's event loop isn't blocked.
 Workers don't receive requests: the main thread runs the server and delegates heavy tasks.
 A thread's uncaught error is handled with worker.on("error") on the main thread;
 worker.on("exit") fires however the thread ended, and is where a respawn would go.
